    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Is this a good offer?\n\n**It is a solid baseline initial offer for a Google L6 PM, but it is not compelling enough for you to accept as-is.**\n\n* **The Base is a step backward:** At Meta you make $240,000 base. Google is offering $235,000, despite their posted band reaching up to **$271,000**.\n* **Total Compensation (TC) is virtually lateral:** \n  * Your current Meta TC is **~$526,000/yr**.\n  * Google\u2019s offer sits around **~$552,000/yr** (assuming an annualized $270,000 equity split). \n  * A ~5% bump is not worth leaving a team you enjoy, resetting your internal reputation, and taking on onboarding risk.\n* **Missing Sign-on Bonus:** Google frequently uses sign-on bonuses ($50k\u2013$100k+) for L6 hires, especially when poaching from Meta. Offering $0 is standard for an initial anchor, not a final number.\n* **The \"Refresher\" math is hypothetical:** While Google's refresher compounding is real over a 4-year horizon, Meta also awards strong annual refreshes. You should not trade guaranteed day-one compensation for hypothetical future performance grants.\n* **Vesting Schedule Nuance:** Note that Google typically front-loads new-hire GSU grants (**38% Year 1, 32% Year 2, 20% Year 3, 10% Year 4**). If this grant is front-loaded, your Year 1 cash flow would be roughly ~$692k ($235k base + $47k bonus + $410k equity), but your Year 4 equity from this initial grant would drop to $108k. Clarify with the recruiter whether this offer is front-loaded or straight-line (25%/yr).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Decision: Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n**Negotiate.** \n\nYou are in a position of maximum leverage:\n1. Google reached out to you; you were not actively looking.\n2. You are happy and well-compensated at Meta.\n3. You have a direct, credible market alternative (staying at Meta for >$520k).\n4. Tech companies expect senior PM candidates (L6/IC6) to counter.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Ask For (Your Negotiation Target)\n\n| Component | Google Initial Offer | Your Target Counter | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | Must beat your current $240k base; well within their $271k top-of-band. |\n| **Equity (4-yr)** | $1,080,000 | **$1,250,000 \u2013 $1,300,000** | Shifts annualized equity from ~$270k to ~$312k\u2013$325k/yr. |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Offsets transition friction and bridges unvested momentum at Meta. |\n| **Year 1 Target TC** | ~$552,000 | **~$640,000 \u2013 $660,000+** | Provides a meaningful ~20\u201325% premium over Meta to justify moving. |\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Structure the Conversation\n\nReach out to the recruiter via phone or email with a clear, collaborative, and firm tone:\n\n> *\"Thank you for the offer. I really enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m genuinely excited about the scope of this L6 role.*\n>\n> *As you know, I wasn't actively looking and I\u2019m in a great position at Meta making ~$526k, with a $240k base and predictable equity vesting. Because of where my current compensation sits, the current offer is essentially lateral and includes a slight decrease in base salary.*\n>\n> *To make the transition compelling and justify walking away from my current trajectory at Meta, I\u2019d be looking for:*\n> 1. *A **base salary of $255,000** (to reflect my current base and experience within the posted band).*\n> 2. *An **equity grant of $1,280,000** over 4 years.*\n> 3. *A **sign-on bonus of $80,000** to offset the transition friction and unvested momentum.*\n>\n> *If we can get the package to these numbers, I\u2019d be ready to sign and fully commit to the team.\"*\n\n### Key Rules of Thumb for This Stage:\n* **Don't bluff about walking away:** You don't need to threaten anything. The simple reality that you are happy at Meta does all the heavy lifting.\n* **Focus on Year 1 & 2 guarantees:** Do not let the recruiter replace guaranteed initial equity/cash with illustrations of future performance refreshers.\n* **Verify the vesting schedule:** Ask explicitly: *\"Is this new-hire grant structured on Google's 38/32/20/10 schedule or straight-line 25% per year?\"*",
